The region between the concentric circles with radii 1 and 2 is shaded in blue. Find the area of the image of the shaded region under the transformation $\ds z\mapsto z+\ds\frac{1}{z}$.



Since we know that the transformation $\ds z\mapsto z+\ds\frac{1}{z}$ can also be considered as $\ds z\mapsto \ds\frac{z^2+1}{z}$, then for any point z inside the circle |z|=1, we get $|\ds\frac{z^2+1}{z}|\geq 2$, thus the image of the circle with radius 1 is outside the circle with radius 2. Also, we can see that the transformation changes angles and shapes of boundaries. The arc of the boundary |z|=1 between points 1 and i maps to the arc of the corresponding boundary between points $1+\ds\frac{1}{1}=2$ and $i-\ds\frac{1}{i}=0$. Thus, using formula $\phi(z)=z+\ds\frac{1}{z}=r(\cos{\theta})+\ds\frac{1}{r}\cos{\theta}+i(r\sin{\theta}-\ds\frac{1}{r}\sin{\theta})=(r+\ds\frac{1}{r})\cos{\theta}+i(r-\ds\frac{1}{r})\sin{\theta}$
we can set up
\begin{equation*}
    \begin{cases}
   (r+\ds\frac{1}{r})\cos{\theta}=0\\
   (r-\ds\frac{1}{r})\sin{\theta}=1
\end{cases}
\end{equation*}
and to solve it for r as a function of $\theta$:

From the first equation we can tell that
$\theta\in (\ds\frac{-\pi}{2},\ds\frac{-\pi}{2})$

Let's compute the following expression using trigonometric pythagorean identity

\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
&(\ds\frac{1}{r}\sin{\theta})^2+(-\ds\frac{1}{r}\cos{\theta})^2=(-1)^2=1\\
&\ds\frac{\sin^2{\theta}+\cos^2{\theta}}{r^2}=\ds\frac{1}{r^2}=1\\
&r^2=1\\
&r=\pm1
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}

Let's replace $r=1$ and $r=-1$ into our equations to check if both of them work.

For $r=1$:

\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
   (1+\ds\frac{1}{1})\cos{\theta}&=0\\
   \cos{\theta}&=0\\
   \theta&=\ds\frac{\pi}{2}+\pi n
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
   (1-\ds\frac{1}{1})\sin{\theta}&=1\\
   0\sin{\theta}&=1\\
   \text{False}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}

We can see that $r=1$ doesn't satisfy the second equation.

For $r=-1$:

\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
   (-1+\ds\frac{1}{-1})\cos{\theta}&=0\\
   -2\cos{\theta}&=0\\
   \cos{\theta}&=0\\
   \theta&=\ds\frac{\pi}{2}+\pi n
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
   (-1-\ds\frac{1}{-1})\sin{\theta}&=1\\
   -2\sin{\theta}&=1\\
   \sin{\theta}&=\ds\frac{-1}{2}\\
   \theta&=\ds\frac{7\pi}{6}+2\pi n\\
   \theta&=\ds\frac{11\pi}{6}+2\pi n
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}

We can see that $r=-1$ satisfies the first equation but doesn't satisfy the second equation.

Let's replace r in the given equations using formulas: $x=r\cos{\theta}$ and $y=r\sin{\theta}$

\begin{equation*}
\begin{cases}
   (r+\ds\frac{1}{r})\cos{\theta}=0\\
   (r-\ds\frac{1}{r})\sin{\theta}=1
\end{cases}
\longrightarrow
\begin{cases}
   (\ds\frac{x^2+y^2}{x}+\ds\frac{x}{x^2+y^2})\ds\frac{x}{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}=0\\
   (\ds\frac{x^2+y^2}{y}-\ds\frac{y}{x^2+y^2})\ds\frac{y}{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}=1
\end{cases}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
(\ds\frac{x^2+y^2}{x}+\ds\frac{x}{x^2+y^2})\ds\frac{x}{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}=0\\
(x^2+y^2)^2+x^2=0\\
\text{since } x^2+y^2>0 \forall (x,y)\neq(0,0) \text{ then}\\
x^2+y^2+x=0
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}

and 

\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
(\ds\frac{x^2+y^2}{y}-\ds\frac{y}{x^2+y^2})\ds\frac{y}{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}=1\\
(x^2+y^2)^2-y^2=y\sqrt{x^2+y^2}\\
\text{or}\\
((x^2+y^2)+y)((x^2+y^2)-y)=y\sqrt{x^2+y^2}\\
(x^2+y^2)^2-y^2=y\sqrt{x^2+y^2}
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}

Let's find the intersections between curve  $(x^2+y^2)^2-y^2=y\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$ and straight line x = 0 to be able to compute area under curve.

Setting $x=0$ gives us $(y^2)^2-y^2=y\sqrt{(y^2)}$

(y^2)^2-y^2=|y|y

Case 1. Assuming that y > 0:

\begin{equation*}
\begin{aligned}
y^4-y^2=y^2\\
y^4-2y^2=y^2(y^2-2)=0\\
y^2=0 \hspace{10mm} \text{or} \hspace{10mm} y^2=2
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}

