Let $f(x) = x^4 + ax^3 - x^2 + ax + 1$ where $a$ is a constant. If $r_1$, $r_2$, $r_3$, $r_4$ are the four roots of $f(x)$, then Vieta's formulas tell us that $$\begin{gather*}
r_1+r_2+r_3+r_4=-a\\
r_1 r_2+r_1 r_3+r_1 r_4+r_2 r_3+r_2 r_4+r_3 r_4=-1\\
r_1 r_2 r_3+r_1 r_2 r_4+r_1 r_3 r_4+r_2 r_3 r_4=a\\
r_1 r_2 r_3 r_4=1
\end{gather*}$$If any of the roots is real, say $r_1$, then we see immediately that the other roots must satisfy the equations $$\begin{gather*}
r_2+r_3+r_4=-a-r_1\\
r_2 r_3+r_2 r_4+r_3 r_4=-1-r_1(-a-r_1)\\
r_2 r_3 r_4=\frac{1}{r_1}
\end{gather*}$$If all of the roots are real, then by Vieta's formulas, the polynomial $$g(x)=x^3 + (-a-r_1)x^2 + (-1-r_1(-a-r_1))x + \frac{1}{r_1}$$must have three real roots. However, we know that a monic cubic polynomial $p(x)$ has either one or three real roots by Descartes' Rule of Signs, since its derivative is a quadratic polynomial and has either 0, 1, or 2 real roots, and a root of $p'(x)$ can be either a local maximum or minimum of the graph of $p(x)$. In addition, observe that a polynomial has three real roots if and only if its derivative has two real roots, in which case it is a local minimum between two consecutive real roots of the polynomial, and the other root of the polynomial must occur before the first real root or after the last real root. Thus the answer is that $f(x)$ has all four roots real if and only if the polynomial $g'(x)$ has 2 real roots, in which case it must satisfy one of these conditions: \begin{enumerate}
\item Both $g''(x)$ and $g(x)$ have no real roots, in which case the graph of $g(x)$ has a single inflection point which is also the global minimum, and the polynomial has exactly one real root which occurs after this inflection point.
\item Both $g''(x)$ and $g(x)$ have 2 real roots, in which case the graph of $g(x)$ has an inflection point between the roots of $g(x)$ which is a local maximum, and $g(x)$ has three real roots.
\end{enumerate}
Therefore, to finish this proof, it suffices to determine the range of values of $a$ for which the two following conditions hold: (i) the discriminant of $g''(x)$ is positive, so it has two real roots; and (ii) either the discriminant of $g(x)$ is negative, so it has no real roots, or it has two real roots. Observe that if we were able to do so in general, we would have obtained a much more general result about the distribution of roots of quartic polynomials with real coefficients. The problem is that it appears we cannot actually do this, because the roots of $g(x)$ involve $r_1$, which we have assumed is real without specifying its value. We could still obtain results for specific values of $a$ by substituting them into $f(x)$ and seeing if the resulting function satisfies our conditions. At least we can easily check whether its roots are real or complex by graphing or using numerical methods, though unfortunately this will not allow us to determine their exact values.
