Consider the parametric equations $x=f(t+a)$ and $y=g(t)$, which are essentially the same except that the parameter $t$ has been shifted by $a$ units. What are the values of $a$ for which the curves defined by these new equations are symmetric about the line $y=x$?

The expression $0.\overline{ab}$ has the meaning that it is a number with decimals $a$ and $b$ which is being repeated. This means the given expression can be written in the form: \begin{align*}
0.\overline{ab}&=ab.ab.ab.ab...\\
&=ab\cdot 0.\overline{01}\\
&=ab\cdot \frac{1}{99}.
\end{align*}
where it was used that $0.\overline{01} = \frac{1}{99}$.

Next, let's consider when $a$ and $b$ are not both nine. In this case we can see that \begin{align*}
0.\overline{ab}&=\frac{10a+b}{99}.
\end{align*}
When $10a+b$ is a multiple of 11, the denominator will be equal to $9$ instead of $99$. In the other cases the denominator will be equal to $99$.

Now let's consider when $a$ and $b$ are not both zero. This gives the following possibilities: \begin{align*}
(1) \text{ Only } a = 0 \\
(2) \text{ Only } b = 0\\
(3) \text{ Both } a, b \neq 0
\end{align*}
We know that: \begin{align*}
(10a+b) \text{ is multiple of } 11 &\iff b-a \text{ is a multiple of } 11.
\end{align*}
Case (1): Let's consider the case when only $a$ is zero, so $b \neq 0$. In this case we get: \begin{align*}
10 \cdot 0 + b = b,
\end{align*}
so it follows that $b$ can not be a multiple of $11$ because $0 < b < 11$. So there is no possibility here.
Case (2): Let's consider the case when only $b$ is zero, so $a \neq 0$. In this case we get: \begin{align*}
10a + 0 = 10a,
\end{align*}
so it follows that $10a$ can be a multiple of $11$, or in other words, $a$ should be a multiple of $11$. Again we see that this can not happen because $0 < a < 11$.
Case (3): Finally let's consider the case when $a$ and $b$ are not zero. Since $b-a$ should be a multiple of $11$ we can distinguish between the following cases: \begin{align*}
(3.1) b - a > 0 \\
(3.2) b - a < 0 \\
(3.3) b - a = 0 \\
\end{align*}
Let's consider these three cases separately.
Case (3.1): $b-a$ is positive and a multiple of $11$. We know that $0 < b < 10$, $0 < a < 10$, and that $a$ and $b$ are integers so it follows that: \begin{align*}
0 < b - a < 10,
\end{align*}
so there are no possibilities here because $b-a$ can never be a multiple of $11$.
Case (3.2): $b - a$ is negative and a multiple of 11. Similarly to above we see that there are no possibilities here because: \begin{align*}
-10 < b-a < 0.
\end{align*}
Case (3.3): $b-a$ is equal to zero. Then it follows that $a=b$ so $10a+b$ will be equal to $11a$. Because $a$ can be any integer between $1$ and $9$ we see that this case gives $9$ possible solutions.
We conclude that for $a$ and $b$ not both nine and not both zero, only $9$ different denominators are possible in the expression $\frac{10a+b}{99}$.

Now let's consider when $a$ and $b$ are not both nine. It can be shown that when $a=b=9$, the denominator will also be equal to $9$. 

It now follows that in total $5$ different denominators are possible in the expression $0.\overline{ab}$. \\


