The values of $x$ which satisfy $f(f(x))=3$ are $x=-3$, $x=1$, and $x=5$.
Let $g(x)=f(f(x))$. Then $g(-3)=g(1)=g(5)=3$, and $g(-1)=1$, $g(3)=5$.
So if we graph $y=g(x)$, then the graph of $y=g(x)$ passes through the points $(-3,3)$, $(1,3)$, $(5,3)$, $(-1,1)$, and $(3,5)$.
This information is enough to plot the graph of $y=g(x)$.
Below, the graph of $y=g(x)$ is shown in red, and the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown in blue. [asy]
