\end{align*}Assuming the system has a unique solution $(x,y)$, find $a$ if the ratio $\frac{x}{y}$ is equal to the ratio $\frac{c}{d}$.

When will this system have a unique solution?
\begin{align*}
\begin{vmatrix}
1&a\\
3&-2a
\end{vmatrix} \ne 0
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
-2a-3a \ne 0
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
-5a \ne 0
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
a \ne 0
\end{align*}
If the system has a unique solution, then $a \ne 0$.

The ratio $\frac{x}{y}$ is equal to the ratio $\frac{c}{d}$. This means $\frac{x}{y}=\frac{c}{d}$.

We can use the first equation to express $x$ in terms of $y$, $x= c-ay$, and then substitute $x$ in the ratio $\frac{x}{y}$, $\frac{c-ay}{y}=\frac{c}{d}$.
