\frac{c(d-y)}{d} = ay
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
\frac{c(d-y)}{dy} = a
\end{align*}

Now we can use the second equation to express $x$ in terms of $y$, $x = \frac{d+2ay}{3}$, and then substitute $x$ in the ratio $\frac{x}{y}$, $\frac{\frac{d+2ay}{3}}{y}=\frac{c}{d}$.
\begin{align*}
d+2ay = \frac{3cy}{d}
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
2ay = \frac{3cy}{d}-d
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
2ay = \frac{3cy-d^2}{d}
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
a = \frac{3cy-d^2}{2dy}
\end{align*}

Now we can equate the two expressions for $a$ to each other, $\frac{c(d-y)}{dy} = \frac{3cy-d^2}{2dy}$.
